{"attachments":[],"author":{"display_name":"DM radar","id":"313689699627696139","is_bot":0,"username":"bfdm"},"author_id":"313689699627696139","channel_id":"746818810954121236","content":"This year has taught me that it is an absolute blast at time to get to collaborate with other dms. But that telling competing story solidifies cliques and drama. In order to changes things I'd like there to be a single narrative with a more open world feel where each of you take on your own borough to help design it and then run the interactions that happen there in game.","created_at":"2020-08-22T20:08:16.104000Z","edited_at":null,"id":"746823073835253850","reply_to_id":null,"thread":null,"thread_id":null}
{"attachments":[],"author":{"display_name":"hekiryuu","id":"237577351532249110","is_bot":0,"username":"hekiryuu"},"author_id":"237577351532249110","channel_id":"746818810954121236","content":"I am suggesting we look at how larps manage DMs and players.  Head DM whose job is to have the overall story and basically say in what the goal/tone of the game is.  Then each other DM have areas of specialization and purpose (for instance, I am generally great with rules but someone like Web has me beat every effing day, and thus many general rules questions would be best suited for them).  It would take a bit to actually figure out who goes where, but in the end could make life a lot easier and less chaotic overall (Larps being designed to manage 20-100 players at once).  Anyway, worth debating.","created_at":"2020-08-22T21:14:04.285000Z","edited_at":null,"id":"746839633706614806","reply_to_id":null,"thread":null,"thread_id":null}
